    /* kb2-paired. The comparison method, shown once for all three changes.

       THE TIE CELL IS DASHED AND HAS NO EDGE OUT OF IT. McNemar's exact test
       reads the discordant pairs and nothing else, so the absence is the claim,
       and it is shown as an absence and stated in the caption as well.

       A marker does not inherit the referencing path's stroke, so this plate
       declares its own. */
    .kb2-plate .kb2-box {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--rule-strong);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .kb2-plate .kb2-tie {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1;
      stroke-dasharray: 4 3;
    }
    .kb2-plate .kb2-edge {
      fill: none;
      stroke: var(--muted);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
    .kb2-plate marker path { fill: var(--muted); }

    /* kb3-quantum. A COPY of the discordant-pair grammar.

       THE ORIGIN is src/pages/projects/faithfulness-judge.astro at bd00680,
       the `fj2-discordant` block at lines 164 to 202. That comment names the
       four things a copy changes and nothing else: the prefix, the two row
       labels, the verdict word, and a new definition in the PLOTS registry of
       scripts/build-plots.cjs with this page's own artifact, its own commit and
       its own key. This page never reads `fj2-discordant`, because those counts
       belong to that study.

       WHY THE MARKS ARE GENERATED. The count IS the geometry here: one mark per
       discordant pair. A typed row of marks is a published count with no
       producer, which is what ADR-013 refuses. build-plots.cjs reads the counts
       out of a pinned artifact and writes the x of every mark into
       src/data/figures.json, so a row cannot drift from the measurement.

       WHY ONE SLOT IS ONE QUERY HERE. The origin sizes its mark at a fixed 12
       units. This copy sizes it at the track divided by the pairs the gold set
       scores, so the mark's width IS the percentage points one query is worth.
       The whole scale therefore comes out of the denominator and not out of a
       hand, which is what the figure argues.

       WHY THE SECOND ROW IS EMPTY. Nothing ran the other way. That is a
       measured zero and not an absence, so the row is shown as it measured and
       the caption states the count.

       WHY THE TWO ROWS SHARE A LEFT EDGE. The reader compares two lengths, not
       two positions. The fill and the row label dual-encode the direction, so
       the plate needs no second hue. */
    .kb3-plate .kb3-rule {
      stroke: var(--border);
      stroke-width: 1;
    }
    .kb3-plate .kb3-win {
      fill: var(--text);
      stroke: none;
    }
    .kb3-plate .kb3-loss {
      fill: var(--bg);
      stroke: var(--text);
      stroke-width: 1.5;
    }
  </style>
